1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,480
This is Doing and Feeling, a four-part listening guide to intelligence and consciousness. Episode 1, Doing.

2
00:00:07,880 --> 00:00:18,100
A quick note before we start. This series was written by Claude, an AI model made by Anthropic, for one listener, and both of the voices you're hearing are synthetic.

3
00:00:18,780 --> 00:00:20,620
It's built from the material you sent.

4
00:00:20,800 --> 00:00:24,000
A book and a lecture by Blaise Aguera e Arcus,

5
00:00:24,340 --> 00:00:29,040
a thread on X where people traded their best recommendations for understanding consciousness,

6
00:00:29,340 --> 00:00:32,320
and an AI-written summary of that thread from Grok.

7
00:00:32,480 --> 00:00:40,220
The answers ran from a 16-page essay about bats to a 600-page textbook that one person said takes a year to finish.

8
00:00:40,560 --> 00:00:47,400
There was a meme about microtubules, a split verdict on Girdle, Escher, Bach, Masterpiece, or Trash,

9
00:00:48,100 --> 00:00:53,580
reply that just said, you're conscious, pay attention for a day. And that's the problem.

10
00:00:53,820 --> 00:00:59,320
I have a reading list as long as my arm and maybe one free weekend. So what's the plan?

11
00:00:59,880 --> 00:01:06,040
Four episodes, about 15 minutes each. This one is about intelligence, what it is, and how scientists

12
00:01:06,040 --> 00:01:11,920
actually study it. Episode two is the hard problem of consciousness and a map of the philosophical

13
00:01:11,920 --> 00:01:17,560
positions. Episode 3 is the science, the experiments, the measurements, and the theories

14
00:01:17,560 --> 00:01:23,700
that compete. And Episode 4 puts it all together. Could an AI be conscious? And what should you read

15
00:01:23,700 --> 00:01:30,040
in what order? Why start with intelligence if the thread was about consciousness? Because the most

16
00:01:30,040 --> 00:01:34,920
common confusion in that thread, and in public debate generally, is treating them as one thing.

17
00:01:35,360 --> 00:01:40,920
The neuroscientist Anil Seth stresses this distinction constantly. Intelligence is about

18
00:01:42,100 --> 00:01:47,840
solving problems and reaching goals. Consciousness is about whether there's any experience going on,

19
00:01:48,040 --> 00:01:53,700
whether there is something it feels like from the inside to be you, doing and feeling, hence the

20
00:01:53,700 --> 00:01:59,340
title. Those could come apart. A system might be extremely good at doing things with nothing felt

21
00:01:59,340 --> 00:02:05,580
at all, and something might feel without being very clever. A newborn baby, a fish in pain,

22
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:09,820
whether they really do come apart is one of the deepest questions in this series,

23
00:02:09,820 --> 00:02:13,760
so we need a clear picture of each one before we ask how they connect.

24
00:02:14,260 --> 00:02:18,240
Okay, so intelligence. Is there even an agreed definition?

25
00:02:18,640 --> 00:02:22,240
There are roughly three traditions, and they define it differently.

26
00:02:22,540 --> 00:02:26,400
The oldest is psychometrics, the science of mental testing.

27
00:02:26,740 --> 00:02:31,080
In 1904, the psychologist Charles Spearman noticed something odd.

28
00:02:31,340 --> 00:02:34,540
Children who did well in one area tended to do well in others,

29
00:02:34,760 --> 00:02:37,780
school subjects like classics, French and math,

30
00:02:37,780 --> 00:02:40,640
and even tests of telling musical pitches apart.

31
00:02:40,960 --> 00:02:43,440
All the scores correlated positively.

32
00:02:44,200 --> 00:02:46,880
Psychologists now call that the positive manifold.

33
00:02:47,620 --> 00:02:50,500
Spearman proposed a single general factor behind it,

34
00:02:50,640 --> 00:02:52,360
which later became known as G.

35
00:02:52,840 --> 00:02:54,120
More than a century later,

36
00:02:54,440 --> 00:02:57,960
that finding has replicated about as well as anything in psychology.

37
00:02:58,420 --> 00:03:00,440
Modern models are hierarchical.

38
00:03:00,800 --> 00:03:02,840
General intelligence sits at the top.

39
00:03:03,140 --> 00:03:04,840
Below it are broad abilities,

40
00:03:04,840 --> 00:03:10,020
like fluid reasoning, accumulated knowledge, processing speed, and working memory.

41
00:03:10,200 --> 00:03:12,520
And below those are narrow skills.

42
00:03:12,860 --> 00:03:18,540
Does G actually predict anything, or is it just an artifact of how tests are built?

43
00:03:18,900 --> 00:03:23,840
It predicts a lot. School performance, job performance, even health and longevity.

44
00:03:24,160 --> 00:03:27,280
Those are statistical tendencies, not destiny.

45
00:03:27,680 --> 00:03:31,340
Twin studies find that the heritability of IQ rises with age,

46
00:03:31,340 --> 00:03:36,940
from modest in early childhood to something like 60 to 80 percent of the differences between adults.

47
00:03:37,220 --> 00:03:43,100
That's counterintuitive. One explanation is that as we get older, we choose environments that suit

48
00:03:43,100 --> 00:03:49,620
our dispositions, which amplifies them. And then there's the Flynn effect. Average IQ scores rose

49
00:03:49,620 --> 00:03:54,960
by roughly three points per decade through much of the 20th century. That's far too fast for

50
00:03:54,960 --> 00:04:01,060
genetics, so environment clearly matters enormously. Schooling, nutrition, a more abstract

51
00:04:01,680 --> 00:04:06,020
In some countries, the rise has recently stalled or even reversed.

52
00:04:06,320 --> 00:04:09,200
But that's all about how humans differ from each other.

53
00:04:09,400 --> 00:04:11,460
It's not intelligence as a thing.

54
00:04:12,100 --> 00:04:13,460
Exactly the limitation.

55
00:04:14,400 --> 00:04:16,440
Psychometrics tells you how people compare.

56
00:04:16,740 --> 00:04:21,780
It does not tell you what an octopus, a crow, or a language model has or lacks.

57
00:04:22,140 --> 00:04:25,060
That said, people have started borrowing it for AI.

58
00:04:25,060 --> 00:04:39,520
In late 2025, a large group of researchers led by Dan Hendricks proposed defining AGI as matching a well-educated adult across 10 ability areas taken from that same hierarchical model.

59
00:04:39,720 --> 00:04:48,640
They scored GPT-4 at about a quarter of the way there and GPT-5 at a little over half, with long-term memory the weakest area.

60
00:04:49,140 --> 00:04:55,500
And a 2026 analysis found that AI benchmark scores show a positive manifold of their own,

61
00:04:55,680 --> 00:04:58,680
though it weakened as models became more specialized.

62
00:04:59,060 --> 00:05:03,420
For a definition that is not anchored to humans, you need the second tradition,

63
00:05:03,740 --> 00:05:05,480
which comes from computer science.

64
00:05:05,740 --> 00:05:10,660
In 2007, Shane Legge, who later co-founded DeepMind,

65
00:05:10,800 --> 00:05:16,240
and Marcus Hutter gathered around 70 definitions of intelligence and boiled them down to one.

66
00:05:16,240 --> 00:05:21,980
Intelligence measures an agent's ability to achieve goals in a wide range of environments.

67
00:05:22,340 --> 00:05:23,980
They even turned it into math.

68
00:05:24,220 --> 00:05:27,840
Score an agent across every possible computable environment,

69
00:05:28,080 --> 00:05:30,280
giving simpler environments more weight.

70
00:05:30,500 --> 00:05:34,060
You cannot actually compute it, but it captures the core idea.

71
00:05:34,620 --> 00:05:37,720
Intelligence is about generality, not one trick.

72
00:05:38,080 --> 00:05:40,460
So a chess engine is not intelligent.

73
00:05:40,760 --> 00:05:42,140
It's just good at chess.

74
00:05:42,440 --> 00:05:44,400
That's Francois Chalet's point.

75
00:05:46,240 --> 00:05:51,260
published a paper called on the measure of intelligence, arguing that skill is not intelligence.

76
00:05:51,660 --> 00:05:57,800
You can buy skill with enough data or by hard coding it. Intelligence is how efficiently you

77
00:05:57,800 --> 00:06:03,400
acquire new skills, how much you can generalize from how little experience, given what you started

78
00:06:03,400 --> 00:06:09,660
with. He built a benchmark around that idea called ARC. It's a set of small colored grid puzzles,

79
00:06:09,660 --> 00:06:16,480
each one knew, that most people solve from a few examples. For years, AI systems did terribly on

80
00:06:16,480 --> 00:06:22,660
it, even while scoring brilliantly on standardized exams. The first two versions have now largely

81
00:06:22,660 --> 00:06:29,080
fallen to the latest models. The newest version, launched in 2026, is a set of small interactive

82
00:06:29,080 --> 00:06:35,380
games with no instructions. At launch, people solved all of them, while Frontier AI systems

83
00:06:35,380 --> 00:06:41,520
scored close to zero. The best systems have since climbed a long way, though how far depends a lot

84
00:06:41,520 --> 00:06:48,240
on exactly how they're tested. And the third tradition? Prediction. This one runs from Herman

85
00:06:48,240 --> 00:06:54,280
von Helmholtz in the 19th century, who described perception as unconscious inference, the brain

86
00:06:54,280 --> 00:06:59,980
guessing at the causes of its sensations. It runs through to modern predictive processing in

87
00:06:59,980 --> 00:07:06,680
neuroscience associated with people like Carl Friston, Andy Clark, and Anil Seth. On this view,

88
00:07:06,880 --> 00:07:11,800
a brain is fundamentally a prediction machine. It builds a model of the world and of its own body,

89
00:07:12,180 --> 00:07:17,040
predicts its incoming signals, and updates itself when it's wrong. And there's a parallel in

90
00:07:17,040 --> 00:07:22,460
computer science. To compress data well, you have to predict it well. And to predict it well,

91
00:07:22,660 --> 00:07:27,220
you have to model whatever generated it. That's why some researchers treat compression

92
00:07:27,220 --> 00:07:28,680
as a test of intelligence.

93
00:07:29,140 --> 00:07:32,660
There's even a prize for compressing a large chunk of Wikipedia.

94
00:07:33,160 --> 00:07:35,180
Which brings us to the book you sent.

95
00:07:35,720 --> 00:07:36,260
Right.

96
00:07:36,580 --> 00:07:40,100
Blaise Aguera E. Arcus is a vice president and fellow at Google,

97
00:07:40,240 --> 00:07:43,420
where he founded a research group called Paradigms of Intelligence.

98
00:07:43,840 --> 00:07:47,720
His book, What is Intelligence?, came out in September 2025

99
00:07:47,720 --> 00:07:50,680
from MIT Press in the Antikythera series.

100
00:07:50,980 --> 00:07:54,920
It's over 600 pages, and the whole thing is free to read online.

101
00:07:55,390 --> 00:07:57,640
The lecture you have is from the same project.

102
00:07:58,040 --> 00:08:01,380
It's about 80 minutes, recorded in London in 2024.

103
00:08:01,960 --> 00:08:04,240
Give me the argument in one breath.

104
00:08:04,660 --> 00:08:06,220
Life is computation.

105
00:08:06,720 --> 00:08:08,200
Intelligence is prediction.

106
00:08:08,540 --> 00:08:11,680
So modern AI is not a fake version of intelligence.

107
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:14,000
It's a new branch of the same tree.

108
00:08:14,260 --> 00:08:16,440
He starts with the origin of life.

109
00:08:16,640 --> 00:08:20,820
In 2024, his team ran an artificial life experiment.

110
00:08:21,100 --> 00:08:24,260
They filled a kind of digital soup with random programs,

111
00:08:24,920 --> 00:08:29,740
in a tiny programming language and let them interact and overwrite each other at random.

112
00:08:29,940 --> 00:08:36,100
There was no fitness function and no goal. In a large fraction of runs, self-replicating programs

113
00:08:36,100 --> 00:08:41,640
appeared on their own and took over the soup, even with random mutations switched off. For him,

114
00:08:41,960 --> 00:08:47,300
that shows life and computation bootstrapping together. The moment something copies itself,

115
00:08:47,600 --> 00:08:51,000
it's computing. And how does he get from there to intelligence?

116
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:54,580
An organism survives by predicting its future,

117
00:08:54,580 --> 00:08:59,120
and that includes predicting other organisms that are busy predicting it back.

118
00:08:59,460 --> 00:09:03,000
That mutual modeling, he argues, drove an arms race.

119
00:09:03,440 --> 00:09:08,580
Social animals model each other's minds and then model themselves with the same machinery.

120
00:09:08,840 --> 00:09:11,640
He also leans hard on symbiogenesis.

121
00:09:11,920 --> 00:09:15,220
That's the idea, championed by Lynn Margiulis,

122
00:09:15,420 --> 00:09:18,180
that major leaps in evolution come from merging.

123
00:09:18,780 --> 00:09:22,460
The classic example is the bacterium that moved into another cell

124
00:09:22,460 --> 00:09:26,400
and became the mitochondria, the power plants inside your cells.

125
00:09:26,660 --> 00:09:31,840
For him, intelligence scales through cooperation and fusion, not only competition.

126
00:09:32,140 --> 00:09:36,720
And he treats large language models as a major evolutionary transition,

127
00:09:37,020 --> 00:09:39,820
systems that learn to predict human language,

128
00:09:39,960 --> 00:09:43,700
and in doing so absorbed a great deal of what language encodes.

129
00:09:44,020 --> 00:09:47,060
So he thinks next word prediction is intelligence.

130
00:09:48,180 --> 00:09:52,540
His introduction says, roughly, that next word prediction done well enough is intelligence.

131
00:09:52,980 --> 00:09:58,540
He also argues that you cannot cleanly separate real intelligence from a perfect simulation of it from the outside.

132
00:09:58,980 --> 00:10:00,360
That's a functionalist view.

133
00:10:00,600 --> 00:10:03,620
What matters is what a system does, not what it's made of.

134
00:10:03,840 --> 00:10:05,400
And he goes further than most.

135
00:10:05,640 --> 00:10:14,360
The publisher's own description says he argues that certain AI systems have a legitimate claim not just to intelligence, but to consciousness and free will.

136
00:10:14,700 --> 00:10:16,360
So is it a good resource?

137
00:10:18,180 --> 00:10:24,160
half of your question, it's one of the best single syntheses around. It covers the origin of life,

138
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:30,060
cybernetics, neuroscience, and the history of neural networks in one argument, from someone

139
00:10:30,060 --> 00:10:35,380
who has built these systems. Two cautions, though. First, it's a thesis, not a survey.

140
00:10:35,600 --> 00:10:40,720
It argues one side of exactly the fight in your thread, so pair it with the strongest version of

141
00:10:40,720 --> 00:10:46,360
the other side, which we'll get to in episode four. Second, it's long. If you're short on time,

142
00:10:48,180 --> 00:10:53,580
Then read the introduction and chapters 6 through 8, which cover consciousness and transformers.

143
00:10:53,840 --> 00:10:59,640
You mentioned octopuses, and Michael Levin came up in the thread with his worms and cells.

144
00:10:59,880 --> 00:11:03,440
Is intelligence outside of brains a serious idea?

145
00:11:03,960 --> 00:11:10,640
Very. Peter Godfrey Smith's book Other Minds, one of the easy classics in your thread, is about the octopus.

146
00:11:11,020 --> 00:11:14,720
It's a mollusk more closely related to a clam than to us,

147
00:11:14,720 --> 00:11:19,580
and it evolved a large nervous system completely independently of vertebrates.

148
00:11:19,760 --> 00:11:23,020
Most of its neurons are in its arms, not its head.

149
00:11:23,220 --> 00:11:26,960
So complex intelligence has evolved at least twice on this planet,

150
00:11:27,160 --> 00:11:28,940
on totally different body plans.

151
00:11:29,260 --> 00:11:32,800
That's strong evidence that intelligence is about organization,

152
00:11:33,180 --> 00:11:35,100
not one particular kind of brain.

153
00:11:35,500 --> 00:11:39,520
Then there's Michael Levin, who studies what he calls basal cognition,

154
00:11:39,940 --> 00:11:42,280
problem-solving below the level of brains.

155
00:11:42,980 --> 00:11:47,580
Cells and tissues use electrical signals to coordinate toward target shapes.

156
00:11:48,100 --> 00:11:52,200
Flatworms called planaria can regrow a whole body from a small fragment.

157
00:11:52,480 --> 00:11:57,620
In one well-known experiment, his lab briefly altered the worm's bioelectric patterns

158
00:11:57,620 --> 00:11:59,640
and produced worms with two heads.

159
00:12:00,040 --> 00:12:04,960
When those worms were cut again, they kept regrowing two heads with no further intervention.

160
00:12:05,400 --> 00:12:07,700
The body's target shape had been rewritten.

161
00:12:08,380 --> 00:12:10,520
Levin treats intelligence as a continuum.

162
00:12:12,280 --> 00:12:17,980
reach the same goal by different routes shows some degree of it. That stretches the word a long way.

163
00:12:18,320 --> 00:12:25,160
It does, and people argue about it. But it forces a useful question. Is intelligence a special thing

164
00:12:25,160 --> 00:12:31,120
that brains do? Or is it a general property of goal-directed systems, which brains happen to do

165
00:12:31,120 --> 00:12:37,440
especially well? How do brains actually build these models? Some of the most concrete answers

166
00:12:37,440 --> 00:12:43,440
come from navigation. In 1971, John O'Keefe found placed cells in the rat hippocampus.

167
00:12:43,760 --> 00:12:50,820
Each one fires when the animal is in a particular spot. In 2005, Maybritt and Edvard Moser's lab

168
00:12:50,820 --> 00:12:56,840
found grid cells nearby, which fire in a hexagonal lattice, like coordinate lines laid over the world.

169
00:12:57,080 --> 00:13:03,320
The three of them shared the Nobel Prize in 2014. It turns out the same machinery maps abstract

170
00:13:03,320 --> 00:13:09,820
spaces too, like relationships and concepts. The Artem Kursanov videos in your list cover this

171
00:13:09,820 --> 00:13:15,860
beautifully, and there's a striking link to AI. Researchers showed that a model of the hippocampus

172
00:13:15,860 --> 00:13:21,820
called the Talman-Ickenbaum machine is closely related, mathematically, to the transformer,

173
00:13:22,120 --> 00:13:28,300
the architecture inside language models. They're not identical, but they're close enough to suggest

174
00:13:28,300 --> 00:13:33,340
that brains and transformers may be converging on similar solutions for learning structure.

175
00:13:33,800 --> 00:13:37,760
Let's talk about the systems everyone's actually arguing about.

176
00:13:37,980 --> 00:13:41,280
What do we know about how language models do what they do?

177
00:13:41,560 --> 00:13:43,620
The short history goes like this.

178
00:13:43,960 --> 00:13:47,060
Decades of trying to hand-code intelligence mostly failed.

179
00:13:47,340 --> 00:13:50,380
What worked was general learning methods, plus scale,

180
00:13:50,620 --> 00:13:52,840
which Rich Sutton later called the bitter lesson.

181
00:13:53,220 --> 00:13:57,140
The key architecture, the transformer, arrived in 2017.

182
00:13:58,300 --> 00:14:03,120
one to predict the next word across a large fraction of the internet, then fine-tune it

183
00:14:03,120 --> 00:14:08,080
with feedback, and you get the systems we have now. The skeptic's line is that it's just

184
00:14:08,080 --> 00:14:14,780
autocomplete, stachastic parrots. That phrase comes from a 2021 paper by Emily Bender and

185
00:14:14,780 --> 00:14:20,100
colleagues, and its core worry is legitimate. Fluent text can be produced without understanding,

186
00:14:20,440 --> 00:14:25,940
but interpretability research has found real internal structure. In one well-known study,

187
00:14:25,940 --> 00:14:30,580
a small transformer trained only on move sequences from the board game Othello

188
00:14:30,580 --> 00:14:32,800
built an internal picture of the board.

189
00:14:33,060 --> 00:14:35,520
The researchers could read that picture out

190
00:14:35,520 --> 00:14:38,460
and even edit it to change which moves the model made.

191
00:14:38,800 --> 00:14:42,660
Larger models represent concepts as directions in their internal space,

192
00:14:42,920 --> 00:14:46,360
track entities through a story, and plan ahead in limited ways.

193
00:14:46,520 --> 00:14:48,720
And the capability results keep coming.

194
00:14:49,160 --> 00:14:54,980
In July 2025, experimental systems from OpenAI and Google DeepMind

195
00:14:55,940 --> 00:15:02,080
gold medal level at the International Mathematical Olympiad, and at this year's Olympiad, two systems

196
00:15:02,080 --> 00:15:08,420
reportedly achieved perfect scores. One research group, Metter, tracks how long a task, measured in

197
00:15:08,420 --> 00:15:14,840
human working time, AI agents can complete on their own. By their estimates, that horizon has

198
00:15:14,840 --> 00:15:19,380
been doubling every few months, though the measurements get shaky for the longest tasks.

199
00:15:19,700 --> 00:15:21,660
So the parent critique is dead.

200
00:15:22,460 --> 00:15:27,380
Reframed more than dead. The open questions are now mostly about how this intelligence is

201
00:15:27,380 --> 00:15:32,220
structured, though skeptics still dispute how deep it goes. These systems are jagged,

202
00:15:32,480 --> 00:15:38,000
superhuman at some tasks and strangely brittle at others. That echoes something called Moravik's

203
00:15:38,000 --> 00:15:43,760
paradox. What's hard for us, like calculus, can be easy for machines, while what's easy for a

204
00:15:43,760 --> 00:15:49,440
toddler, like moving around a cluttered room, is hard. They also learn from vastly more data than

205
00:15:49,440 --> 00:15:55,420
any human ever sees, which by Chalet's measure counts against them. And they are mostly disembodied.

206
00:15:55,640 --> 00:15:59,360
They do not act in the world under the constant pressure of staying alive.

207
00:15:59,700 --> 00:16:03,920
As we'll see, that matters a great deal to some theories of consciousness.

208
00:16:04,400 --> 00:16:07,120
So if you had to compress this episode...

209
00:16:07,540 --> 00:16:13,160
Three points. One, intelligence is the ability to model the world well enough to reach goals

210
00:16:13,160 --> 00:16:17,720
across many situations, and the field increasingly caches that out as prediction,

211
00:16:19,440 --> 00:16:25,160
learn something new. Two, it's not tied to one kind of hardware. It evolved separately in

212
00:16:25,160 --> 00:16:30,960
octopuses and invertebrates, shows up in simple forms in cells and tissues, and now shows up in

213
00:16:30,960 --> 00:16:36,360
machines. And three, which is the hinge for the rest of the series, all of that is about doing.

214
00:16:36,700 --> 00:16:41,560
None of it, on its own, tells you whether there's anything it's like to be the system doing it.

215
00:16:41,880 --> 00:16:45,120
Which brings us to bats. Next episode.

